AP040 test suite
================

Testbenches (Icarus Verilog -g2012 and ModelSim, see run_tests.sh):

  tb_ap040_reset.v      milestone B: reset vector fetch, 16-bit bus adapter
                        sequencing (byte lanes, misaligned word/long), MOVE/
                        MOVEQ/MOVEA/Bcc subset, wait-state robustness
  tb_ap040_program.v    runs a self-checking assembled program twice (back to
                        back ready and varied wait states):
    asm/t_integer.s     milestone C integer ISA: MOVE/MOVEA all EA modes incl.
                        full 68020 extension words and memory indirect, ALU
                        ops with exact CCR checks, ADDX/SUBX/NEGX stickiness,
                        shifts/rotates incl. count 0 and over-width, bit ops,
                        word and long MUL/DIV incl. 64-bit forms and overflow,
                        BCD, EXT/EXTB/SWAP/EXG, LINK/UNLK (.W/.L), JSR/BSR/
                        RTS/RTD, DBcc/Scc, CMPM, TAS, MOVEM, MOVEP, PACK/UNPK,
                        MOVE16, MOVE from/to CCR
  tb_ap040_diagrom.v  boots a real ROM image against a minimal Amiga model
                        (chip RAM, CIA overlay, custom register stubs) and
                        captures serial output. DiagROM V2.0 completes its
                        boot, passes its own 2MB memory address test and
                        detects the core as "CPU: 68LC40 FPU: NONE". Build
                        with Verilator (--binary --timing); iverilog is too
                        slow for hundreds of millions of cycles. ROM image:
                        python3 bin2hex.py DiagROM/diagrom.rom build/diagrom.hex
                        Kickstart 3.1 (A1200 40.68) also runs: exec.library
                        initializes and ExecBase is built and relocated; the
                        FPU F-line probe and the privilege traps from exec's
                        Supervisor() are the only exceptions taken. Boot then
                        parks waiting on CIA timers the stub does not model.
                        Note: ROM images whose header starts $1116 are linked
                        for $200000 (ReKick) and will not boot at $F80000.

    asm/t_mmu.s         milestone E MMU: three-level table walk with U/M
                        history bit updates, page remapping, split I/D ATC
                        caching semantics, PTEST/MMUSR, PFLUSH page and all
                        variants, DTT transparent bypass, and access error
                        (format $7) fault-fix-restart for write protection,
                        invalid data page, instruction fetch, and user access
                        to a supervisor page
    asm/t_cache.s       milestone G caches: CACR enables, I-cache staleness
                        with self-modifying code and CINV recovery, D-cache
                        staleness against DMA-style pokes and CINV recovery,
                        TTR cache-inhibit bypass, byte/word extraction from
                        cached lines, misaligned bypass, disable behavior
    asm/t_exceptions.s  milestone C exceptions: TRAP/illegal/BKPT/A-line/
                        F-line/CHK/divide-by-zero/TRAPV/TRAPcc frames and
                        vector offsets, format $0/$1/$2 layout, RTE format
                        validation and format error, MOVEC full 040 register
                        matrix with write masks, MOVE USP, MOVES with SFC/DFC
                        (FC pins checked by the TB), PTEST/PFLUSH/CINV/CPUSH
                        decode and privilege, user/supervisor round trip over
                        USP, privilege violation, autovectored interrupts,
                        IPL masking, STOP wake-up, NMI edge behavior, and the
                        M-bit master/interrupt stack throwaway frame path

Build requirements: vasmm68k_mot (vbcc toolchain) and python3; see
build_tests.sh. Test programs communicate over memory-mapped registers
($F100 fail number, $F102 result, $F110 IPL request, $F120 FC check).

Known gaps in the core at milestone C (all take an illegal-instruction or
F-line trap unless noted; planned in AP040_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md):

  - CAS2 (bitfields, CAS, CHK2/CMP2 and BTST Dn,#imm are implemented;
    CAS is not bus locked)
  - trace: T1 (all instructions) and T0 (change of flow) generate format
    $2 vector 9 exceptions on completion; an instruction that itself
    raises an exception (TRAP etc) suppresses its trace, and STOP is not
    traced
  - TAS is not bus-locked; table-search descriptor updates are plain
    read/write cycles (single-master fabric)
  - berr input ignored (the wrapper ties it 0)
  - interrupts are always autovectored (the wrapper ties ipl_autovector=1)
  - format $7 frames carry SSW/EA/FA with zeroed writeback slots; faults
    use pure instruction restart with EA register rollback. MOVEM restart
    across a page fault is covered by t_mmu.s (the CM continuation bit is
    never set, which handlers treat as a plain restart)
  - caches are write-through with invalidate-on-write, so CPUSH equals
    CINV and no copyback/push state exists; CINV/CPUSH invalidate the
    whole selected cache regardless of scope (architecturally safe);
    misaligned and line-crossing reads bypass the caches; the I-cache is
    not snooped by CPU writes (real 68040 behavior: software must CINV)
  - the Minimig build sets AP040_ENABLE_CACHE=0 in cpu_wrapper.v: the
    internal caches are compiled out (CINV/CPUSH complete immediately,
    which is a valid 68040 whose caches never fill) and cpu_cache_new in
    the RAM controllers provides snooped caching instead. Simulation
    keeps the caches enabled (compat default is 1) so t_cache.s stays
    meaningful
  - misaligned accesses that fault report MA=0 and restart the whole
    transfer; caches (milestone G) and the FPU (milestone H) are absent;
    invalid translations are not cached in the ATC (each access re-walks)
  - 8K page mode (TC.P) is implemented in RTL but only 4K is covered by
    t_mmu.s so far
